From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anatoliy Shulika <shulikaa@icloud.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: broadcom: bcm4908: add PCIe controllers

BCM4908 has three PCIe controllers. Describe all three (PCIE0 at
0x80040000, PCIE1 at 0x80050000 and PCIE2 at 0x80060000), each with its
own memory window, MSI controller, power domain and reset line. They are
left disabled here; boards enable the ones they populate.

PCIE0 and PCIE1 are tested on a TP-Link Archer C2300 v1. PCIE2 is
unpopulated on that board - the vendor bootlog reports "failed to bring
up core [2] link" - so its node is derived from the vendor SDK headers
and the kernel's own BCM4908 tables but is not runtime-tested. It is
described anyway because it is part of the SoC and other BCM4908 boards
do wire it up.

Signed-off-by: Anatoliy Shulika <shulikaa@icloud.com>
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/broadcom/bcmbca/bcm4908.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/broadcom/bcmbca/bcm4908.dtsi
@@ -313,6 +313,157 @@
 		};
 	};
 
+	/*
+	 * PCIE0 and PCIE1 are confirmed real, populated PCIe links on the
+	 * TP-Link Archer C2300 v1: booting stock firmware shows both
+	 * controllers linking up and enumerating real endpoint devices with
+	 * real BAR assignments (PCIE0 gets an 8MB BAR, consistent with a
+	 * WiFi chip's register/buffer space). PCIE2 is unpopulated on that
+	 * board ("failed to bring up core [2] link" in the stock boot log),
+	 * so its node is described from the vendor headers but has not been
+	 * exercised; boards that populate it enable it themselves.
+	 *
+	 * Register bases/sizes and outbound MMIO windows are from the
+	 * vendor GPL SDK's 4908_map_part.h (PERF_PHYS_BASE-relative
+	 * PCIE0_PHYS_BASE/PCIE0_MEM_PHYS_BASE etc). Interrupt numbers are
+	 * from the same SDK's 4908_intr.h (INTERRUPT_PCIE_0/1_CPU_INTR,
+	 * SPI_TABLE_OFFSET-relative) and were cross-checked against the
+	 * NAND controller's already-known-correct interrupt (SPI 37 here
+	 * matches this DTS's existing nand-controller node exactly),
+	 * confirming the SPI_TABLE_OFFSET+N convention translates directly
+	 * to DTS GIC_SPI N.
+	 *
+	 * No separate MSI interrupt line exists per controller in the
+	 * vendor header (4908_intr.h only defines one _CPU_INTR per PCIE
+	 * core), unlike richer brcmstb SoCs (e.g. bcm2711/RPi4, which wires
+	 * "pcie" and "msi" to two distinct physical GIC lines). The root
+	 * complex decodes MSI writes internally and re-asserts them on its
+	 * own existing interrupt line rather than driving a dedicated pin,
+	 * so one line serves both roles - matching the vendor header
+	 * exactly. A first attempt at enabling this (self-referencing
+	 * msi-parent with no second interrupts entry at all) failed probe
+	 * outright on real hardware ("cannot map MSI interrupt", "probe of
+	 * internal MSI failed") because irq_of_parse_and_map(.., 1) had
+	 * nothing to resolve. Fixed by giving index 1 a real entry that
+	 * reuses the same GIC_SPI line as index 0 under the name "msi",
+	 * since both roles are serviced by the one physical interrupt this
+	 * SoC actually has.
+	 *
+	 * Without a working MSI (or any other) interrupt path, brcmfmac's
+	 * PCIe msgbuf/doorbell completion notification has nothing to wake
+	 * it: confirmed on real hardware by /proc/interrupts showing zero
+	 * entries for GIC_SPI 59/60 at all (compare brcmnand/switch/enet,
+	 * which do show up), while the WiFi chip's firmware was confirmed
+	 * alive (actively producing new console output) but brcmfmac timed
+	 * out on every query and reported "dongle is not responding" -
+	 * consistent with the host never being notified a response was
+	 * ready, regardless of nvram/calibration content (verified by
+	 * testing two very different nvram files with identical failure).
+	 *
+	 * power-domains is not optional in practice, even though the binding
+	 * does not require it: the vendor driver explicitly powers each core
+	 * up via pmc_pcie_power_up() before touching any registers, and the
+	 * mainline pcie-brcmstb.c driver has no equivalent power-domain code
+	 * of its own - it relies entirely on the generic power-domain
+	 * framework powering the device on before probe() runs. Without this
+	 * property the very first register access in brcm_pcie_probe()
+	 * (RGR1_SW_INIT_1, offset 0x9210, well within this node's own reg
+	 * range) takes a synchronous external abort and panics, confirmed by
+	 * testing on real hardware. BCM_PMB_PCIE0/1/2 indices are from
+	 * drivers/pmdomain/bcm/bcm-pmb.c's own bcm4908_pmb_domains table.
+	 *
+	 * reset-names = "perst" is required, not cosmetic: the driver
+	 * looks the reset up by that exact name
+	 * (devm_reset_control_get_optional_exclusive(dev, "perst")).
+	 * Without it, pcie->perst_reset stays NULL, PERST# to the
+	 * downstream device is never deasserted, and the device stays
+	 * held in reset - confirmed on real hardware: the root port
+	 * itself comes up fine, but bus 01 shows no device at all, and
+	 * brcm_pcie_perst_set_4908's WARN_ONCE(!pcie->perst_reset, ...)
+	 * fires during brcm_pcie_start_link.
+	 *
+	 * The reset index is the PCIe core number: it selects a bit in the
+	 * misc block's miscPCIECtrl register (offset 0x44, the
+	 * reset-controller node below), which the vendor driver drives as
+	 * "misc_regs->miscPCIECtrl &= ~(1 << core)" to assert and
+	 * "|= (1 << core)" to release - clear-to-assert, which is why the
+	 * mainline compatible is registered active-low in reset-simple.c.
+	 */
+	pcie0: pcie@80040000 {
+		compatible = "brcm,bcm4908-pcie";
+		reg = <0x0 0x80040000 0x0 0xa000>;
+		device_type = "pci";
+		#address-cells = <3>;
+		#size-cells = <2>;
+		#interrupt-cells = <1>;
+		interrupts = <GIC_SPI 59 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>,
+			     <GIC_SPI 59 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+		interrupt-names = "pcie", "msi";
+		interrupt-map-mask = <0x0 0x0 0x0 0x7>;
+		interrupt-map = <0 0 0 1 &gic GIC_SPI 59 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>,
+				<0 0 0 2 &gic GIC_SPI 59 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>,
+				<0 0 0 3 &gic GIC_SPI 59 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>,
+				<0 0 0 4 &gic GIC_SPI 59 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+		msi-controller;
+		msi-parent = <&pcie0>;
+		power-domains = <&pmb BCM_PMB_PCIE0>;
+		ranges = <0x02000000 0x0 0xc0000000  0x0 0xc0000000  0x0 0x10000000>;
+		dma-ranges = <0x42000000 0x0 0x00000000  0x0 0x00000000  0x0 0x20000000>;
+		resets = <&pcie_reset 0>;
+		reset-names = "perst";
+		status = "disabled";
+	};
+
+	pcie1: pcie@80050000 {
+		compatible = "brcm,bcm4908-pcie";
+		reg = <0x0 0x80050000 0x0 0xa000>;
+		device_type = "pci";
+		#address-cells = <3>;
+		#size-cells = <2>;
+		#interrupt-cells = <1>;
+		interrupts = <GIC_SPI 60 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>,
+			     <GIC_SPI 60 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+		interrupt-names = "pcie", "msi";
+		interrupt-map-mask = <0x0 0x0 0x0 0x7>;
+		interrupt-map = <0 0 0 1 &gic GIC_SPI 60 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>,
+				<0 0 0 2 &gic GIC_SPI 60 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>,
+				<0 0 0 3 &gic GIC_SPI 60 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>,
+				<0 0 0 4 &gic GIC_SPI 60 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+		msi-controller;
+		msi-parent = <&pcie1>;
+		power-domains = <&pmb BCM_PMB_PCIE1>;
+		ranges = <0x02000000 0x0 0xd0000000  0x0 0xd0000000  0x0 0x10000000>;
+		dma-ranges = <0x42000000 0x0 0x00000000  0x0 0x00000000  0x0 0x20000000>;
+		resets = <&pcie_reset 1>;
+		reset-names = "perst";
+		status = "disabled";
+	};
+
+	pcie2: pcie@80060000 {
+		compatible = "brcm,bcm4908-pcie";
+		reg = <0x0 0x80060000 0x0 0xa000>;
+		device_type = "pci";
+		#address-cells = <3>;
+		#size-cells = <2>;
+		#interrupt-cells = <1>;
+		interrupts = <GIC_SPI 61 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>,
+			     <GIC_SPI 61 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+		interrupt-names = "pcie", "msi";
+		interrupt-map-mask = <0x0 0x0 0x0 0x7>;
+		interrupt-map = <0 0 0 1 &gic GIC_SPI 61 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>,
+				<0 0 0 2 &gic GIC_SPI 61 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>,
+				<0 0 0 3 &gic GIC_SPI 61 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>,
+				<0 0 0 4 &gic GIC_SPI 61 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+		msi-controller;
+		msi-parent = <&pcie2>;
+		power-domains = <&pmb BCM_PMB_PCIE2>;
+		ranges = <0x02000000 0x0 0xe0000000  0x0 0xe0000000  0x0 0x10000000>;
+		dma-ranges = <0x42000000 0x0 0x00000000  0x0 0x00000000  0x0 0x20000000>;
+		resets = <&pcie_reset 2>;
+		reset-names = "perst";
+		status = "disabled";
+	};
+
 	bus@ff800000 {
 		compatible = "simple-bus";
 		#address-cells = <1>;
@@ -620,7 +771,7 @@
 			#size-cells = <1>;
 			ranges = <0x00 0x2600 0xe4>;
 
-			reset-controller@2644 {
+			pcie_reset: reset-controller@2644 {
 				compatible = "brcm,bcm4908-misc-pcie-reset";
 				reg = <0x44 0x04>;
 				#reset-cells = <1>;
